    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "# Keep only delivered orders for RFM analysis\n",
        "orders = orders[orders['order_status'] == 'delivered'].copy()"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "LJs3dVbW8X_V"
      },
      "execution_count": 11,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "The order status analysis showed that 97.02% of orders were delivered, while the remaining 2.98% were classified as shipped, cancelled, unavailable, or in other stages of processing.\n",
        "\n",
        "For the RFM analysis, only delivered orders were retained to ensure that Recency, Frequency, and Monetary were calculated using completed transactions. This resulted in 96,478 orders being considered for the analysis."
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "MhtgpA6E8pcu"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "###1.3 Data Preparation Decisions\n",
        "\n",
        "Based on the initial data exploration and quality checks, the following decisions were made to prepare the dataset for RFM analysis.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Order Status:** Only orders with the status `delivered` were included in the analysis. These represent 97.02% of all orders (96,478 out of 99,441). This decision ensures that the RFM metrics are based on completed transactions rather than cancelled or incomplete orders.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Customer Identification:** The `customer_unique_id` was selected to identify individual customers. In the Olist dataset, `customer_id` identifies a customer record associated with an order, while `customer_unique_id` allows multiple orders belonging to the same customer to be grouped together. This distinction is essential for calculating purchase frequency and total spending per customer.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Monetary Value:** Customer spending was calculated using `payment_value` from the order payments dataset. Payments associated with the same customer were summed to calculate their total monetary value, including orders with multiple payment records.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Data Integration:** The orders, customers, and payments datasets were linked using their corresponding identifiers (`order_id` and `customer_id`). These relationships allow Recency, Frequency, and Monetary metrics to be calculated at the individual customer level.\n",
        "\n",
        "These decisions establish a consistent basis for calculating the RFM metrics and performing customer segmentation."
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "AM3gfwea8tEQ"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "## **2. Recency**\n",
        "\n",
        "Recency measures the number of days since a customer's most recent purchase. Since this analysis uses historical transaction data, recency was calculated relative to the latest purchase date available in the filtered dataset, rather than the current date.\n",
        "\n",
        "Customers with more recent purchases receive higher Recency scores, while those who have not purchased for longer receive lower scores."
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "AxZl4LuUuDrC"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "# Select only the columns needed for the analysis\n",
        "orders_subset = orders[['order_id', 'customer_id', 'order_purchase_timestamp']]\n",
